.TH MDV 1 "September 2026" "mdv" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mdv \- render markdown in the terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mdv
.RI [ flags ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mdv
renders CommonMark and GitHub-flavored markdown for reading in a terminal:
headings, emphasis, syntax-highlighted code, box-drawn tables, task lists,
footnotes, clickable links and inline images.
.PP
With no
.IR file ,
or with
.BR \- ,
the document is read from standard input.
Several files are shown one after another, each under a header naming it.
.PP
The inline HTML that READMEs use is understood:
.B <kbd>
keys,
.B <br>
line breaks, including inside table cells,
.B <sub>
and
.BR <sup> ,
the text styles such as
.BR <b> ,
and
.BR <a> ,
.B <img>
and comments.
Other tags, and HTML blocks, are shown as they are written.
.PP
Math written in TeX between dollar signs,
.B $E = mc^2$
inline or
.B $$
on lines of its own, is shown in Unicode where that can spell it: Greek
letters, operators, arrows, and raised and lowered digits, so the example
reads E\~=\~mc\(S2.
What has no such spelling stays as TeX.
A dollar sign followed by a space, or a closing one followed by a digit, is not
math, so prices are left alone; write
.B \e$
for a dollar sign that would otherwise start math.
.PP
When standard output is a terminal,
.B mdv
opens an interactive pager.
Otherwise it writes the document out and exits, and the output contains no
escape sequences at all \(em no color, hyperlinks or images \(em so redirecting
it to a file or piping it into another program gives plain text.
.PP
Where it can,
.B mdv
asks the terminal what it supports rather than guessing: it writes a short
string of queries and reads the replies, to learn whether images can be drawn
and how, the size of a character cell, and the background color that decides
between the dark and light themes.
Color depth comes from
.B COLORTERM
and
.BR TERM ,
and hyperlink support from recognizing the terminal.
.B mdv \-caps
shows what was found.
.SH OPTIONS
Flags take one dash or two, and a value either as the next argument or after
an equals sign:
.B \-width 80
and
.B \-\-width=80
are the same.
Boolean flags are switched off with
.BR \-flag=false .
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-width " \fIcolumns\fR"
Lay the document out this many columns wide.
The default is the terminal's width, capped at 100 columns because prose set
much wider is harder to read; or 80 when output is not a terminal.
.TP
.BI \-theme " name"
Color theme:
.BR dark ,
.BR light ,
.BR plain ,
or
.BR auto ,
the default, which picks dark or light from the terminal's actual background
color.
.TP
.BI \-color " depth"
How many colors to use:
.BR none ,
.BR 16 ,
.BR 256 ,
.BR truecolor ,
.BR always ,
or
.BR auto ,
the default.
Auto uses what the terminal supports, and none when output is not a terminal.
.B always
forces color on even when output is redirected, at the depth the environment
advertises.
.TP
.BI \-links " mode"
How links are shown.
.B inline
prints the destination in parentheses after the link text;
.B hide
shows only the text.
The default,
.BR auto ,
hides destinations when the terminal supports clickable hyperlinks, since the
text itself then carries the link, and shows them otherwise.
A path to a local file is shown relative to the current directory, or under
.IR ~ ,
when that is shorter than the full path.
.TP
.BI \-images " protocol"
How to draw images:
.BR kitty ,
.BR sixel ,
.BR none ,
or
.BR auto ,
the default, which uses whichever the terminal was found to support.
Where no image can be drawn, its alt text is shown instead.
.TP
.B \-remote\-images
Fetch images referenced by http or https URLs.
Off by default, so that reading a document never makes a network request.
.TP
.BI \-pager " mode"
.B auto
pages when output is a terminal and streams otherwise;
.B never
always streams;
.B always
insists on the pager and fails if there is no terminal to draw on.
.TP
.BI \-frontmatter " mode"
What to do with YAML frontmatter.
.BR meta ,
the default, shows it as a compact header of aligned keys and values;
.B hide
drops it;
.B raw
leaves it to be rendered as ordinary markdown.
.TP
.BI \-vault " directory"
Treat this directory as the Obsidian vault the documents belong to.
By default the vault is found automatically; see
.B OBSIDIAN VAULTS
below.
.TP
.B \-no\-vault
Do not resolve wikilinks and embeds against a vault.
.TP
.B \-ascii
Draw tables, rules, bullets and quotes with ASCII characters instead of
Unicode box drawing, for fonts or terminals that lack the glyphs.
.TP
.B \-caps
Report what the terminal supports, and whether each answer was measured or
inferred from the environment, then exit.
The first thing to try when something renders wrongly.
.TP
.B \-no\-probe
Do not query the terminal; decide everything from environment variables.
.TP
.BI \-probe\-timeout " duration"
How long to wait for the terminal to answer its queries, such as
.B 300ms
(the default) or
.BR 1s .
Worth raising over a slow remote connection.
.TP
.BI \-config " file"
Read defaults from this file instead of the usual one.
.TP
.B \-no\-config
Ignore the configuration file.
.TP
.B \-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-help
Print a summary of the flags and exit.
.SH PAGER
In the pager, the document scrolls under a one-line status bar showing its
name and position.
Resizing the window lays the document out again at the new width, keeping the
reading position.
.TP
.BR j ", " k ", " Down ", " Up ", mouse wheel"
Scroll one line.
The wheel works by having the terminal send the arrow keys for it, so the
mouse is not captured and selecting text and clicking links work as usual.
.TP
.BR d ", " u
Scroll half a screen.
.TP
.BR Space ", " b ", " "Page Down" ", " "Page Up" ", " Ctrl\-F ", " Ctrl\-B
Scroll a screen.
.TP
.BR g ", " G ", " Home ", " End
Jump to the start or the end.
.TP
.BR ] ", " [
Jump to the next or previous heading.
.TP
.B t
Show the table of contents, a line per heading with the one being read
selected.
.BR j ,
.BR k ,
the arrows and the other scrolling keys move the selection,
.B Enter
jumps to it, and
.BR Esc ,
.B q
or
.B t
closes it.
With several files, each file's name heads its own headings.
.TP
.B /
Search.
Matches are highlighted as the query is typed, ignoring case;
.B Enter
jumps to the next match and
.B Esc
cancels.
.TP
.BR n ", " N
Jump to the next or previous match.
.TP
.B ?
Show the keys.
Any key closes the list, except the scrolling keys, which scroll it when the
window is too short to show all of it.
.TP
.BR q ", " Ctrl\-C
Quit.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Defaults for any flag can be kept in a configuration file, read from the
first of:
.IP \(bu 3
the file named by
.BR \-config ;
.IP \(bu 3
the file named by
.BR MDV_CONFIG ;
.IP \(bu 3
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/mdv/config ;
.IP \(bu 3
.IR ~/.config/mdv/config .
.PP
The file holds one flag per line, written as
.I name
=
.IR value ,
with the flag's name and no dashes.
A name on its own switches a boolean flag on.
Lines starting with
.B #
are comments; a
.B #
elsewhere is part of the value.
Quotes around a value are optional.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
# ~/.config/mdv/config
theme = light
width = 90
links = inline
ascii
.fi
.RE
.PP
Anything given on the command line wins over the file, so
.B mdv\~\-theme=dark
still gets the dark theme with the file above.
.PP
An unknown name or a bad value in the file is an error that names the line.
.BR \-config ,
.BR \-no\-config ,
.B \-version
and
.B \-caps
cannot be set in the file.
A missing file at the default location is not an error, but a missing file
named by
.B \-config
or
.B MDV_CONFIG
is.
.SH OBSIDIAN VAULTS
A document inside an Obsidian vault \(em a directory holding a
.I .obsidian
folder, found by searching upwards from the document \(em also gets the
syntax Obsidian adds to markdown:
.TP
.B ![[image.png]]
An embedded image, drawn inline.
.B ![[image.png|300]]
caps it at 300 pixels wide.
.TP
.B [[Some Note]]
A link to the note's file.
.B [[Some Note|label]]
shows
.I label
instead, and
.B [[Some Note#Section]]
reads
.IR "Some Note > Section" .
.PP
Embeds are resolved the way Obsidian resolves them: in the attachment folder
set in
.IR .obsidian/app.json ,
then the note's own folder, then the vault root, then anywhere in the vault by
name, preferring the shallowest match.
A link or embed that resolves to nothing still shows its label.
.PP
Three more pieces of Obsidian syntax work in any document, inside a vault or
not:
.TP
.B > [!warning] Title
A callout: a blockquote drawn with a bar and a bold title in the kind's color,
and an icon.
Obsidian's kinds and their aliases are recognized
.RB ( note ,
.BR tip ,
.BR warning ,
.BR danger ,
.BR faq ,
and the rest); unknown kinds are drawn as notes.
GitHub's alerts, such as
.BR ">\~[!NOTE]" ,
use the same syntax and render the same way.
.TP
.B %%comment%%
Hidden, whether inline or spanning several paragraphs between lines holding
only
.BR %% .
Inside code it is left alone, and a
.B %%
that is never closed is shown rather than hiding the rest of the document.
.TP
.B ==highlight==
Drawn on a yellow background.
The markers have to touch the text, so
.B a\~==\~b
is left as it is.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MDV_CONFIG
The configuration file to read.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where the configuration directory is, if not
.IR ~/.config .
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set to anything but the empty string, no color is used, as described at
https://no\-color.org.
.B \-color
still overrides it.
.TP
.BR COLORTERM ", " TERM
The color depth.
.B TERM=dumb
turns color off.
.TP
.B COLORFGBG
Whether the background is dark, when the terminal cannot be asked.
.TP
.BR TMUX ", " STY
Inside tmux or screen the terminal is not probed, because the multiplexer
intercepts the replies, and images are only drawn if
.B \-images
asks for them.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including
.B \-h
and
.BR \-version .
.TP
.B 1
An error: a file could not be read, or the configuration file is invalid.
.TP
.B 2
The command line could not be parsed, or a flag was given a value it does not
accept.
.SH EXAMPLES
Read a file in the pager:
.PP
.RS 4
.B mdv README.md
.RE
.PP
Render into a file as plain text, 72 columns wide:
.PP
.RS 4
.B mdv \-width 72 notes.md > notes.txt
.RE
.PP
Page through several files, keeping color through a pipe into
.BR less (1):
.PP
.RS 4
.B mdv \-color=always *.md | less \-R
.RE
.PP
Render markdown produced by another command:
.PP
.RS 4
.B curl \-s https://example.com/README.md | mdv
.RE
.PP
See what the terminal supports:
.PP
.RS 4
.B mdv \-caps
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR less (1),
.BR kitty (1)
.PP
https://github.com/rafael0rueda/markdown_cli
